 ## Background
-Marching Cubes was introduced by William E. Lorensen and Harvey E. Cline in 1987 [S3, unofficial]. It was designed to create triangle models of constant density surfaces from 3D medical data (CT, MR, SPECT) [S3, unofficial]. The algorithm uses a divide-and-conquer approach to generate inter-slice connectivity and processes data in scan-line order [S3, unofficial]. It is the most common surface fitting algorithm for scalar fields [S1, unofficial].
+Marching Cubes was introduced by William E. Lorensen and Harvey E. Cline in 1987 [S3, unofficial]. It extracts an isosurface from a 3D discrete scalar field $f(x, y, z) = c$ [S19, unofficial]. The algorithm iterates over every cube in the lattice using nested loops [S17, unofficial]. It is the most common surface fitting algorithm for scalar fields [S1, unofficial].
 
